# Which node and wallet images this deploy runs (#247).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The z3-stack compose file is a separate upstream project
# checked out beside ours, and its image lines carry defaults. With no override we
# inherited somebody else's default, so the version of the node our funds depend on
# was not a PR in this repo, had no review gate, and `docker inspect` on the box was
# the only way to find out what we ran. The July 28 advisory described old Zebra
# nodes halting silently at an activation height; we were fine, and we only knew
# that because a person checked by hand.
#
# HOW IT IS USED. deploy/deploy.sh reads this file and writes the image lines into
# the docker-compose.override.yml it generates for the z3 clone (#269), which is the
# same file the miner address goes into and for the same reason: the upstream clone
# stays pristine so a rebuild is reproducible. An explicit `image:` beats the
# upstream compose file's default, which is the point.
#
# This section used to say install-ops.sh copied this file to `.env` beside the
# compose file. IT DID NOT, and nothing else did either: grep for Z3_ZEBRA_IMAGE
# before #269 returns audit-drift.sh and its test, nothing that runs anything. So
# the documented route was fiction and a version bump changed only what the audit
# EXPECTED, which is worse than an unpinned version because it reads as covered.
#
# CHANGING A VERSION IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. The override has to be regenerated
# and the stack recreated, and today that means re-running deploy/deploy.sh on the
# box, which is safe to re-run and brings the components back up itself. The
# 2-minute auto-deploy timer ships THIS repo only and never touches the z3 clone,
# so a drift line after an edit here is expected until someone does that. Not
# REDEPLOY.md, which covers the app container and not the node stack.

# Zebra, the consensus node. Version state format matters for snapshot import:
# v6.0.0, v6.2.0 and the fork all declare state format 28.0.0 (see SNAPSHOTS.md),
# so those are interchangeable for a restore. Do not assume that holds across a
# major without checking zebra-state/src/constants.rs.
#
# 6.2.0 -> 6.2.3 (#169). zebra-state/src/constants.rs was fetched at all four tags in
# the 6.2.x line and is BYTE-IDENTICAL across them, declaring 28.0.0, so a snapshot
# taken on 6.2.0 restores into 6.2.3. Checked because the alternative is discovering it
# during a restore.
#
# The jump is more than the peer hardening the release title advertises, and two of the
# changes land on code paths we own:
#   6.2.1  Testnet getblocktemplate no longer switches to a minimum-difficulty template
#          EARLY. On testnet the old behaviour future-dated the timestamp and produced
#          spurious minimum-difficulty blocks that depress difficulty below equilibrium.
#          That is our miner, on our network, on the version we are running today.
#   6.2.2  getblock, getblockheader and gettxout now bind their follow-up state queries
#          to the hash the first read resolved, so a reorg mid-call can no longer return
#          an internally inconsistent answer. Our chain-identity check compares a block
#          hash at a common height, so that inconsistency is exactly what would have
#          read as a fork.
#   6.2.2  peer disk-cache write retried every 20s instead of waiting 5 minutes.
#   6.2.3  peer-set hardening: outbound slots no longer held by peers that cannot serve
#          blocks, dropped connections replaced, and no penalising peers for briefly
#          disagreeing about the NU6.3 activation. That last one is the failure that
#          stranded a shield and crash-looped the wallet here.
#
# NOT fixed by this bump, so nobody reads it as a general upgrade: getblocktemplate still
# panics the node when the chain is below Heartwood activation, identically in 6.2.0 and
# 6.2.3 (#166). Does not affect production, which is far past Heartwood.
Z3_ZEBRA_IMAGE=zfnd/zebra:6.2.3

# The wallet. This is a THIRD-PARTY build (zodlinc), not a Zcash Foundation or ECC
# one, which is #184's finding and the reason it is written down here rather than
# left implicit. Hard gate: no third-party wallet build against mainnet funds.
#
# Shipped unset in #266 because the running tag is only knowable from the box, and
# guessing it would have put a wrong number under review. Read off the box by the CTO
# with the command below, so this line is a report of what runs, not a preference.
#   docker inspect -f '{{.Config.Image}}' "$(docker ps --filter name=zallet --format '{{.Names}}' | head -n1)"
#
# PINNED BY DIGEST, and the digest is not decoration. z3's own compose file defaults
# this to the same tag WITH this digest attached, so writing the tag alone here would
# have replaced an immutable reference with a moveable one: a tag can be re-pushed and
# a digest cannot. For a third-party wallet build that holds the faucet's funds (#184)
# that is the wrong direction to move, and it would have happened silently, as our
# value overrides theirs.
#
# Verified from here, not assumed: `docker buildx imagetools inspect` on the tag returns
# index digest sha256:1849b446..., the same one upstream pins, so the tag and the digest
# are the same image today. That is exactly why adding the digest is safe right now and
# why it is worth doing before the tag moves.
#
# audit-drift.sh compares digests rather than reference text, so a container created
# from the plain tag still reads as a match instead of as permanent drift.
Z3_ZALLET_IMAGE=zodlinc/zallet:v0.1.0-beta.1@sha256:1849b4469875dc0165942c06d15fa6a7da76b2d43bade578cc8e5903a639869d

# Zaino, the light-client indexer. Nothing to pin by default: the overlay does not
# start a standalone indexer and points at a public Zaino for read-side lookups
# (see README.md). Set this only if you self-host it with --profile indexer.
#Z3_ZAINO_IMAGE=
